{t:"hayek",q:"Marx and Hayek both, per the exit ticket, are…",o:["epistemological relativists","ontological realists","sociological institutionalists","communicative theorists"],a:1,e:"Both assume a real world; they differ epistemologically."},
// cozz
{t:"cozz",q:"Cozzolino (2020) is best described as…",o:["an empirical quantitative study of Bergamo","a conceptual article with illustrative examples","a historical institutionalist case study","a Marxist critique"],a:1,e:"Tutorial 1: conceptual, Bergamo and Oosterwold illustrations."},
{t:"cozz",q:"Which normative conception best fits Cozzolino's preference for autonomy, distributed control and anti-paternalism?",o:["Happy City","Equal City","Supportive City","Free City"],a:3,e:"Tutorial 1 slide."},
{t:"cozz",q:"A critic says: 'What emerges spontaneously in adaptive neighbourhoods may just raise exchange value for owners rather than use value for residents.' This critique draws on…",o:["rational choice","a Marxist / political economy perspective","Hayek","the governmental model"],a:1,e:"Markets as power structures; exchange vs use value."},
// gov
{t:"gov",q:"In Rydin's governmental model, the goals of planning…",o:["are set by planners using expertise","stand outside the planning process and are set politically","emerge from stakeholder consensus","are irrelevant"],a:1,e:"Weberian separation."},
{t:"gov",q:"Which is NOT a source of market failure named by Rydin (2021)?",o:["Externalities","Public goods","Monopoly of landowners","Path dependency"],a:3,e:"Path dependency is a HI concept."},
{t:"gov",q:"For the governmental model, the legitimacy of plans comes primarily from…",o:["public participation","the rationality of the analysis by planners","market outcomes","tradition"],a:1,e:"Rydin p. 24."},
{t:"gov",q:"Where does the governmental model sit on the knowledge axes?",o:["Situated, pluralist, relativist","Universal, monist, realist","Situated, monist, relativist","Universal, pluralist, relativist"],a:1,e:"Knowledge gathered 'in a positive way'."},
{t:"gov",q:"Which research question most clearly takes the governmental approach?",o:["How do tenants experience renovation-driven displacement?","Which policy instrument most effectively increases affordable housing output in municipality X?","How do discourses of 'liveability' legitimise demolition?","How did capital accumulation shape the waterfront?"],a:1,e:"Problem-solving, instrumental, goals given."},
{t:"gov",q:"Implementation studies (Rydin 2021) showed that…",o:["policy instruments work automatically","planners exercise discretion and negotiate, and goals are reformulated in implementation","implementation is unimportant","planners never influence goals"],a:1,e:"Blurs the Weberian line."},
{t:"gov",q:"Which critique targets the assumption of 'clear goals agreed by all'?",o:["Rittel & Webber: no definitive problem formulation","Lindblom: ends and means are chosen simultaneously; interests conflict","Weber: bureaucracy","Lichfield: 11 steps"],a:1,e:"Lindblom (and Davidoff) challenge agreed goals."},
{t:"gov",q:"The Westerscheldetunnel MKBA illustrates that…",o:["cost-benefit analysis decides policy instead of politicians","not all effects can be expressed in euros; MKBA informs but politicians decide","the tunnel should keep its toll","the rational model is abandoned in NL"],a:1,e:"'The feeling of injustice about the toll'."},
// scott
{t:"scott",q:"Scott argues that the unplanned Brasília was…",o:["an accident soon removed","symbiotic with the planned city: the order of the centre required it","planned by Le Corbusier","smaller than the planned city"],a:1,e:"75% of residents lived there by 1980."},
{t:"scott",q:"Jane Jacobs's central critique, as presented by Scott, is that…",o:["cities need more highways","visual geometric order does not correspond to functional, experienced order","zoning should be stricter","planners lacked data"],a:1,e:"Visual vs experienced order."},
{t:"scott",q:"'Legibility' in Scott means…",o:["clear typography in plans","the state simplifying society/space so it can be read and controlled from above","participatory transparency","readability of laws"],a:1,e:"Core Scott concept."},
{t:"scott",q:"Which statement about high modernism's knowledge assumptions is correct?",o:["It valued mētis","It assumed universal scientific knowledge monopolised by experts","It was pluralist","It was relativist"],a:1,e:"Universal + monist."},
{t:"scott",q:"Scott's own critique of high modernism is closest to which knowledge position?",o:["Universal and monist","Situated and pluralist (valuing local practical knowledge)","Positivist","Rational choice"],a:1,e:"Mētis and street-level knowledge."},
// davoudi
{t:"davoudi",q:"Davoudi (2006) argues that 'evidence-based planning'…",o:["is the reality of planning","is largely rhetoric; evidence is contested and used in interactive, value-laden ways, so 'evidence-informed' is more realistic","should be replaced by intuition","only applies to transport"],a:1,e:"Rhetoric vs reality."},
{t:"davoudi",q:"'Planners do not uncover facts like geologists but organise facts as evidence within arguments, like lawyers.' This reflects a…",o:["positivist position","post-positivist position","Marxist position","rational choice position"],a:1,e:"Exit ticket: Hoch via Davoudi."},
// alex
{t:"alex",q:"Alexander (2000) defines rationality as having reasons and giving reasons. 'Giving reasons' refers to…",o:["means–ends calculation","justifying a course of action to others","maximising utility","consensus"],a:1,e:"Justification."},
{t:"alex",q:"According to Alexander, classic rationality and Habermas's communicative action are…",o:["identical","mirror images: each covers what the other neglects (deliberation vs interaction)","both descriptive","both substantive"],a:1,e:"Basis of his contingency framework."},
{t:"alex",q:"Planning schools keep teaching the rational model with only token acknowledgement of its problems. Alexander calls this the…",o:["search response","ritual response","abandonment response","avoidance response"],a:1,e:"Ritual."},
// rc
{t:"rc",q:"What distinguishes rational choice theory from rational-comprehensive planning?",o:["RCT assumes only planners are rational","RCT is an analytical framework assuming all actors pursue self-interest, including state actors (state failure)","RCT rejects property rights","RCT is value-free expert planning"],a:1,e:"Lecture comparison slide."},
{t:"rc",q:"A homeowner values quiet at €1,000; insulating the factory costs €700. With zero transaction costs, Coase predicts…",o:["insulation only if the homeowner holds the right","insulation only if the factory holds the right","insulation in both cases; only who pays whom differs","no insulation"],a:2,e:"Efficiency is independent of the initial allocation."},
{t:"rc",q:"Which is a Coasean-inspired approach to Airbnb externalities?",o:["A blanket ban on short-term rentals","Tradable permits for hosting nights","Demolishing tourist areas","Ignoring externalities"],a:1,e:"Creates a market by defining rights."},
{t:"rc",q:"Why does Airbnb regulation lag despite collective harm, in Olson's terms?",o:["Neighbours are a small, concentrated group","Hosts/investors are small, concentrated and organised; neighbours are large and diffuse and free-ride","Planners are irrational","Tourists vote"],a:1,e:"Collective action problem."},
{t:"rc",q:"Rational choice: statements. I: Residents who would collectively benefit from stopping a development will likely organise successfully. II: A small group of landowners may have more influence than many tenants. III: Planners can facilitate cooperation by reducing transaction costs. IV: The public interest is an objective outcome planners can determine independently.",o:["I T, II T, III T, IV F","I F, II T, III T, IV F","I F, II T, III F, IV T","I T, II F, III T, IV F"],a:1,e:"The midterm sample question: I free-riding (F), II Olson (T), III Coase (T), IV aggregate of interests (F)."},
{t:"rc",q:"Which describes the 'betterment/compensation asymmetry'?",o:["Owners pay for both gains and losses","Owners keep value increases from planning but claim compensation for decreases","The state keeps all gains","Tenants receive the betterment"],a:1,e:"Lecture slide on rent."},
{t:"rc",q:"Which is NOT a critique of rational choice theory from the lecture?",o:["Oversimplistic model of behaviour","Ignores power and inequality","Too focused on structure","Limiting assumptions like zero transaction costs"],a:2,e:"The critique is that it is too focused on individuals (agency)."},
{t:"rc",q:"In rational choice theory, knowledge is generally approached as…",o:["situated and pluralist","positivist: universal rational behaviour, generalisable models","relativist","communicative"],a:1,e:"Core assumption 5/6."},
{t:"rc",q:"Why, on Bouwmeester's hypothesis, does densification work in Switzerland?",o:["Swiss planners are more rational","Land law excludes outside speculators and caps farmland price gains, so insiders gain from inward growth","Switzerland has no landowners","Densification is subsidised"],a:1,e:"Peasant Land Act (BGBB)."},
// needham / olson / taylor
{t:"needham",q:"'Land-use planning will succeed only if it can influence the private decisions' (Needham, p. 24). This implies…",o:["planning can command development directly","landowners hold an effective veto, so planning works through incentives and rights","planning is purely communicative","property rights are irrelevant"],a:1,e:"Needham 2012, p. 24."},
{t:"needham",q:"'Property rights can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use' shows that rational-choice approaches can explain…",o:["only reproduction","transformation through deliberate redesign of rules","only emergent change","nothing about change"],a:1,e:"Lecture: Needham = transformation, Taylor = reproduction."},
{t:"olson",q:"Olson's 'selective incentives' are…",o:["public goods for all","private benefits/sanctions only for (non-)members that make contributing rational","tax breaks for developers","planning permits"],a:1,e:"Excludable rewards/punishments for (non-)contributors (Hindmoor p. 116)."},
{t:"taylor",q:"In Taylor (2016), landowners inside the growth boundary who lobby to keep growth restricted act rationally because…",o:["they love farmland","scarcity keeps their land rents high","they are forced by law","they want lower prices"],a:1,e:"Rent-seeking."},
{t:"taylor",q:"Taylor's finding that 'landowner-led pressure… led to modifications to the GAIC bill' illustrates…",o:["communicative consensus","rent-seeking by concentrated interests shaping value-capture rules","Marxist revolution","spontaneous order"],a:1,e:"Rational choice rent-seeking."},
// ni
{t:"ni",q:"E.R. Alexander defines institutional design as the deliberate creation of institutions by planners to achieve politically defined goals. How would a sociological institutionalist critique this?",o:["Institutions can be designed with strong coalitions and resources","Institutions are embedded in cultural norms and cognitive scripts that shape planners' own perceptions and limit top-down design","Institutions change mainly at critical junctures after external shocks","Institutions emerge through evolutionary trial and error beyond deliberate control"],a:1,e:"Midterm example Q1. C = historical institutionalism, D = Moroni's evolutionary view."},
{t:"ni",q:"In Dembski & Salet's typology, historical institutionalism treats institutions as…",o:["external and validated in action","internalised and existing prior to action","external and prior to action","internalised and validated in action"],a:1,e:"Table 1."},
{t:"ni",q:"Which concept belongs to rational choice institutionalism?",o:["Logic of appropriateness","Habitus","Transaction costs","Critical junctures"],a:2,e:"North, Williamson."},
{t:"ni",q:"Institutions vs organisations (North): which is an organisation?",o:["Property rights","The Omgevingswet's rules","A housing corporation","The norm of queuing"],a:2,e:"Organisations are the players."},
{t:"ni",q:"'People follow the rule because that is what is appropriate here, not because it pays off.' This is characteristic of…",o:["rational choice institutionalism","sociological institutionalism","the governmental model","Olson"],a:1,e:"Logic of appropriateness (March & Olsen)."},
// dembski
{t:"dembski",q:"According to Dembski & Salet, why did the 'Flemish Diamond' fail to change policy?",o:["The metaphor was badly designed","Its meaning was not institutionally embedded/inculcated among actors","Too little money","It aimed at preservation"],a:1,e:"Success lies in institutional embedding, not the symbol."},
{t:"dembski",q:"Dembski & Salet position themselves in which quadrant?",o:["External / prior to action","Internal / prior to action","External / in action","Internal / in action (sociological)"],a:3,e:"Internalised and validated in action."},
{t:"dembski",q:"Why do Dembski & Salet reject historical institutionalism for their purpose?",o:["It ignores history","Its focus on continuity/path dependency makes it weak at explaining transformation","It is too quantitative","It ignores the state"],a:1,e:"They want the transformative potential of institutions."},
{t:"dembski",q:"Bourdieu's rites of institution, in Dembski & Salet, show that…",o:["rituals are irrelevant to planning","consecrating an arbitrary boundary can transform reality by changing representations ('social magic')","institutions never change","symbols work regardless of audience"],a:1,e:"Discontinuity out of continuity; 'become what you are'."},
{t:"dembski",q:"On structure/agency, Dembski & Salet (via Bourdieu's habitus) hold that…",o:["structure determines action mechanically","agency is free of structure","practice is informed by internalised structure but not determined by it","only material structure matters"],a:2,e:"They seek to bridge objectivism and subjectivism."},
{t:"dembski",q:"Which statement would Dembski & Salet reject?",o:["Institutions are not carved in stone","Symbolic markers can be issued voluntaristically by skilled planners","Institutions must be validated repeatedly in practice","The audience must inculcate a symbol's meaning"],a:1,e:"'Symbolic markers cannot be used in a voluntaristic manner'."},
// moroni
{t:"moroni",q:"Moroni's 'second-order taxis-oriented approach' refers to…",o:["detailed land-use end-state plans","engineeristic redesign of the institutional framework itself for specific goals","spontaneous evolution of rules","communicative planning"],a:1,e:"First-order = activities; second-order = institutions."},
{t:"moroni",q:"For Moroni, most institutions are…",o:["natural phenomena","artificial phenomena created by deliberate design","artificial phenomena resulting unintentionally from interacting intentional actions","organisations"],a:2,e:"Ferguson: 'result of human action, but not of human design'."},
{t:"moroni",q:"Moroni proposes to replace 'institutional design' with…",o:["'strategic planning'","'organisational design' and 'institutional intervention'","'communicative action'","'critical junctures'"],a:1,e:"Section 3.1."},
{t:"moroni",q:"Which is NOT one of Moroni's kosmos-oriented principles?",o:["Gradualism","Robustness","Allocative efficiency","Openness"],a:2,e:"He replaces allocative with ADAPTIVE efficiency."},
{t:"moroni",q:"Where does Moroni sit on 'deliberate ↔ emergent'?",o:["Strongly deliberate","Strongly emergent (evolutionary)","Neutral","Determined by structure"],a:1,e:"Institutions evolve unintentionally; reform only modest."},
{t:"moroni",q:"Moroni's view is intellectually closest to…",o:["Marx","Hayek and the Scottish Enlightenment","Le Corbusier","Habermas"],a:1,e:"Kosmos/taxis is Hayek's distinction."},
{t:"moroni",q:"Jane Jacobs's explanation of safe streets and Schelling's segregation model are, for Moroni, examples of…",o:["nomic explanations","invisible-hand explanations","rational planning","historical institutionalism"],a:1,e:"Section 2.3."},
// sorensen
{t:"sorensen",q:"Sorensen defines a critical juncture (after Capoccia & Kelemen) as…",o:["a long period of stable institutions","a relatively short period with a substantially heightened probability that agents' choices affect the outcome","any change in law","a crisis"],a:1,e:"Crisis alone = permissive condition only."},
{t:"sorensen",q:"The postwar housing crisis in Ontario (1940s) functioned, in Sorensen's terms, as a…",o:["productive condition","permissive condition","reactive sequence","developmental pathway"],a:1,e:"Crisis opens the window; productive conditions shape the outcome."},
{t:"sorensen",q:"According to Sorensen, path dependence in urban institutions usually means…",o:["complete lock-in","continuous contestation and incremental change within a bounded developmental pathway","no change at all","random change"],a:1,e:"Discussion section."},
{t:"sorensen",q:"Why, per Sorensen, did three housing-crisis junctures in Toronto have different outcomes (two strengthened LVC, one weakened it)?",o:["Different permissive conditions","Different productive conditions (actors, ideology, legal structures, timing)","Random chance","Federal law"],a:1,e:"Both conditions needed; productive ones decided."},
{t:"sorensen",q:"What does Sorensen say historical institutionalism adds to conjunctural analysis?",o:["A focus on global ideology only","Mid-level concepts and methods to analyse contestation over specific institutions, actors and change mechanisms","A rational choice model","Communicative ethics"],a:1,e:"HI clarifies the 'particular' side."},
{t:"sorensen",q:"Sorensen's normative commitment is most clearly…",o:["efficiency","social equity in distributing costs and benefits of development","liberty","aesthetic quality"],a:1,e:"LVC as an equity metric."},
{t:"sorensen",q:"An introduction states: 'once actors have ventured far down a particular path, they are likely to find it very difficult to reverse course.' Which perspective?",o:["Rational-comprehensive planning","Historical institutionalism (path dependency)","Communicative planning","Sociological institutionalism"],a:1,e:"Midterm sample Q3 excerpt."},
// cross-cutting
{t:"ni",q:"Which author would most likely say 'planning strictu senso cannot be applied to institutions'?",o:["Alexander","Moroni","Sorensen","Rydin"],a:1,e:"Moroni's closing observation."},
{t:"gov",q:"Which theory treats planners as experts choosing means and ALSO assumes value-free decisions, while rational choice theory treats planners as…",o:["neutral experts","self-interested actors like everyone else","irrelevant","revolutionaries"],a:1,e:"State failure is possible in RC."},
{t:"know",q:"Which theory/author pairing is WRONG?",o:["Communicative turn: Healey, Innes","Wicked problems: Rittel & Webber","Muddling through: Davidoff","Advocacy planning: Davidoff"],a:2,e:"Muddling through is Lindblom (1959)."},
{t:"scott",q:"Moroni's 'taxis-oriented approach' and Scott's 'high modernism' share the critique that…",o:["planners use too little data","engineer-like design ignores dispersed/practical knowledge and complexity","markets fail","institutions are internalised"],a:1,e:"Both draw on Hayek/Oakeshott-type arguments about practical knowledge."},
{t:"hayek",q:"Which pair is correctly matched with a normative commitment?",o:["Hayek: material equality","Marx: equality of rights","Buitelaar's Free City: liberty","Rational comprehensive: democracy"],a:2,e:"Free City = liberal."}
