const OPEN = [
{t:"ni",pts:15,q:"Read this excerpt (from the midterm guidelines): 'the paper builds on Lowndes' (2009) analogy of a game by devising a novel typology of the different players that shape both formal and informal rules… offers a dynamic approach to history, giving agency to all kinds of social groups (North, 1992). It also provides critical insights into why once actors have ventured far down a particular path, they are likely to find it very difficult to reverse course (Reinalda, 2013)… reinforcing a particular socio-political path into the future.' (a) Which theoretical perspective do the authors adopt? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each with a direct quote. (10)",
model:"(a) Neo-institutionalism, specifically historical institutionalism (with actor-centred elements: formal and informal rules, players).\n(b) Argument 1, institutions as rules of the game: they analyse the 'formal and informal rules and practices' shaped by 'players', using Lowndes' 'analogy of a game' and citing North (institutions = rules of the game). Argument 2, path dependency: 'once actors have ventured far down a particular path, they are likely to find it very difficult to reverse course' and 'reinforcing a particular socio-political path into the future', which explains why the status quo (informality) endures despite pressure for change: reproduction via vested interests.",
rubric:["Names neo-institutionalism / historical institutionalism","Argument 1 about rules (formal/informal) + quote","Argument 2 about path dependency / reproduction + quote","Explains HOW each quote shows the perspective (not just quotes)"]},
{t:"know",pts:12,q:"A municipality organises a large participation process on a new neighbourhood and states that 'the residents' experience is the knowledge base of the plan'. Using Rydin (2007), evaluate this statement. Use the concepts universal/situated, monist/pluralist and opening up/closing down.",
model:"The statement is pluralist (residents are recognised as knowledge producers) and situated (local experience is valid knowledge). Rydin supports opening up to such claims after the communicative turn, but warns that experience 'is not of itself a sufficient condition for it to be knowledge' (p. 56): claims must be tested (closing down), and knowledges are not additive, so consensus does not guarantee the best knowledge. The planner should also co-produce knowledge and combine experiential claims with empirical, predictive and processual claims (e.g. traffic or flood risk). So: legitimate as opening up, insufficient without closing down.",
rubric:["Correctly classifies as pluralist + situated","Uses opening up AND closing down","Uses the Collins & Evans point (experience ≠ automatically knowledge)","Gives a concrete example of testing a claim"]},
{t:"gov",pts:12,q:"Explain the governmental (rational public administration) model of planning according to Rydin (2021). Then give two critiques from different theorists and state which assumption each critique targets.",
model:"Model: planning justified by market failure (welfare economics); politicians set goals outside the process (Weber); planners bring expert knowledge to choose means; a linear rational process with feedback (Lichfield's 11 steps); evidence-based, a comprehensive synthesis, legitimacy from the rationality of analysis (p. 24); research = policy evaluation, a 'critical friend'. Critique 1: Rittel & Webber: wicked problems have no definitive formulation, so complete information is impossible (targets complete information). Critique 2: Lindblom: ends and means are chosen together through incremental bargaining (targets clear, agreed goals). Alternative: Davidoff: planners aren't neutral, plural interests (targets value-free decisions / separation of politics).",
rubric:["Welfare-economic justification + public interest","Politics sets goals / planners choose means (Weber)","Rational process steps or evidence/synthesis","Two named critiques linked to specific assumptions"]},
{t:"hayek",pts:10,q:"Compare Marx and Hayek on (a) structure vs agency, (b) how change occurs, and (c) the role of the planner.",
model:"(a) Marx: structure dominant; the base (means/relations of production) determines the superstructure; deterministic. Hayek: agency/individualism; society is too complex to know or steer. (b) Marx: change via class struggle and deliberate revolutionary transformation of the base (abolish private property). Hayek: change is emergent: spontaneous order from individuals acting under general rules. (c) Marxist planner: redistributes towards material equality, decommodified housing (Madden & Marcuse). Hayekian planner: sets framework rules, equality of rights, avoids end-state steering (Glaeser & Gyourko: zoning reform).",
rubric:["Structure (Marx) vs agency (Hayek)","Deliberate/revolution vs emergent/spontaneous order","Material equality vs equality of rights/liberty","Planner role for each with example"]},
{t:"moroni",pts:12,q:"A planning scholar proposes to 'redesign the Dutch land-use institutions from scratch to maximise housing production'. How would Moroni (2010) respond? And how would Dembski & Salet (2010) respond differently?",
model:"Moroni: this is a second-order taxis-oriented (engineeristic) approach, treating institutions like organisations. Institutions largely evolved unintentionally (human action, not design) and hold dispersed knowledge. There is never a tabula rasa; reform should follow kosmos principles: modesty, gradualism, robustness, openness, adaptive (not allocative) efficiency, general rules. Dembski & Salet: institutions are internalised and must be validated in action; change is possible and deliberate, but only if new meanings are inculcated and embedded in existing norms and habitus. A blueprint won't work 'voluntaristically'; find institutional voids, use legitimate symbolic markers. Difference: Moroni stresses emergent evolution and limits of knowledge (Hayekian); D&S stress cultural meaning and see deliberate transformation as possible.",
rubric:["Moroni: taxis/second-order + evolutionary nature of institutions","At least 2 kosmos principles","D&S: internalised, validated in action, embedding/inculcation","States the contrast (emergent vs deliberate but embedded)"]},
{t:"sorensen",pts:10,q:"Using Sorensen (2024), explain the concepts critical juncture, permissive and productive conditions, and reactive sequence with the Toronto land value capture case.",
model:"Critical juncture: short period of heightened probability that agents' choices shape outcomes (Capoccia & Kelemen), e.g. the 1946 Planning Act. Permissive conditions: crises that loosen constraints (postwar housing crisis; 1980s real-estate bubble; post-2010 affordability crisis). Productive conditions: actors, legal structures, ideology, timing that shape the outcome, e.g. a conservative government allowing subdivision agreements in 1946, the DC Act 1989, and in 2019–22 a pro-supply provincial government cutting LVC (Bills 108/23). Reactive sequence: subsequent contestation (court challenges, tribunal appeals, revisions in 1997 and later) within a developmental pathway: path dependence without lock-in.",
rubric:["Correct CJ definition","Permissive vs productive distinction with Toronto example","Reactive sequence + example","Path dependence as bounded pathway"]},
{t:"rc",pts:10,q:"Analyse the problem of short-term rental (Airbnb) in Amsterdam from a rational choice perspective. Use Olson and Coase, and name one critique of this approach.",
model:"Actors are self-interested: hosts earn more than from long-term tenants, guests get cheap, flexible stays, neighbours bear externalities (noise, housing pressure). Olson: hosts/investors are a small concentrated group that organises and lobbies; neighbours are large and diffuse and free-ride, so regulation lags. Regulation faces enforcement and transaction costs. Coase: with clear rights and zero transaction costs, bargaining would be efficient; since TC > 0, clarify rights and reduce TC: tradable night permits, VvE bargaining, a hosting tax into a neighbourhood fund, platform data sharing. Critique: ignores power and inequality (tenants can't bargain), or an oversimplified human model.",
rubric:["Identifies actors' incentives","Olson collective action applied","Coase + transaction costs + Coasean instrument","One critique"]},
{t:"scott",pts:10,q:"Explain why Scott calls Brasília a high-modernist experiment and what its failures reveal about high modernism's assumptions about knowledge.",
model:"High modernism: faith in science and the state to rationally redesign society. Brasília was built from zero on state land according to CIAM/Le Corbusier doctrines: geometry, functional segregation, superquadra, a utopian miniaturisation meant to transform Brazil. Failures: brasilite (no street corners, anonymity), disorientation for residents (legible to administrators, confusing to users), and the unplanned Brasília housing 75% of residents by 1980, symbiotic with the core. Knowledge: it assumed universal, expert, monist knowledge seen from above and ignored local practical knowledge (mētis) and experienced order (Jacobs). Visual order ≠ functional order.",
rubric:["Defines high modernism","Describes Brasília plan features","Two failures incl. unplanned Brasília","Link to universal/monist knowledge vs mētis/Jacobs"]}
];
